# The Fire Code Already Wrote MWAA's Intelligibility Standard

*Humanized draft — Stage 3. Prepared for airport executives, capital planners, and policy readers. Article with executive summary. Bracketed [VERIFY: …] tags mark claims the Fact-checker must resolve against primary sources.*

---

## Executive summary

The Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority is about to procure the replacement of Dulles Concourses C and D. Trade press puts the announced IAD transformation above 5 million square feet [VERIFY: sq-ft vs. July 29, 2026 release] and above $20 billion, on capital MWAA has not yet fully financed.[^1] The capital plan also contemplates an eventual replacement of the DCA Terminal 1 concourse, on no published schedule.[^11] So the question before the Board is not whether to write a "quiet airport" policy. The question is whether MWAA binds an intelligibility standard into the Design Manual before the Concourse C/D consultant scope hardens — or binds it later, as change orders, on airline-recoverable rates already forecast to carry IAD signatory cost per enplanement from a $12.77 base toward $20.71 by 2030.[^2]

The instrument exists. The Design Manual, "a mandatory guide with the force of law on the airport property" in MWAA's own words, updates annually.[^3] Whether it already carries binding STI, LEQ, RT60, or PA-zoning language is not public [VERIFY: current Design Manual acoustic contents]. That diligence read is the first deliverable.[^12]

The numbers exist too. NFPA 72 sets voice-alarm intelligibility at average STI ≥ 0.50 per Acoustically Distinguishable Space and ≥ 0.45 at 90% of measurement locations, verified occupied per IEC 60268-16 (STIPA); ACRP 175 adds signal-to-noise ≥ 10 dB(A) for pier departure lounges.[^4] The accessibility floor is federal. 49 CFR 27.71 makes visual paging a condition of grant eligibility, 14 CFR 382.53 obliges carriers to give passengers with vision or hearing impairment the same information as everyone else,[^5] and Part 382 rulemaking is moving toward more real-time information, not less.[^13]

What the evidence ledger does not carry — and this article will not sell — is peer-reviewed proof that a "silent airport" reduces missed boardings or IROPS recovery time. London City, Helsinki, Schiphol, and Barcelona publish governance rules, not performance numbers.[^6] Even San Francisco's headline is a mechanism story: 47% fewer paging events and 60% less announcement duration, achieved by disabling airline access to boarding-area paging.[^7]

The recommendation is therefore not a "quiet-by-design standard" — a flag that lands sideways at post-January-29 DCA — but a **Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard**: a mandatory intelligibility floor; redundant channels (audible, visual/digital, staffed) for every safety-critical and boarding-critical message; PA governance with an IROPS carve-out; a tenant-systems chapter reaching airline podium audio and concession televisions; and a two-airport variance clause. Priced components come to $6M–$24M one-time, $40M as a conservative envelope with unpriced commissioning and PA head-end work — roughly $0.02–$0.09 per IAD enplanement, or 0.10–0.43 percent of projected 2030 signatory CPE.[^8] The sponsor is the SVP for Engineering, under existing standards-maintenance authority, with a 90-day plan and two stop conditions. This is a Board action inside the current officer term, not a study.

