## Fort Lauderdale, January 6, 2017, ninety minutes after the first shot

Five people were killed and six wounded at initial contact. Ninety minutes later, false reports of a second shooter rippled through Terminal 2, and passengers ran. The stampede put more than fifty additional people in the hospital. The National Policing Institute's after-action review records a chaotic self-evacuation across all four terminals;[^9] independent reporting added radio failures across the responding agencies.[^9a]

Fort Lauderdale-Hollywood appears in no silent-airport case study. It should be the first one. The injuries in hour two came from the absence of an intelligible, authoritative voice reaching a crowd already in motion — a reading that is this article's, not the after-action report's. And every airport that lets its acoustic environment assemble by accretion, one PA head-end, one airline podium, one concession television at a time, is running the same experiment.

## The peer canon has no numbers; the fire code has all of them

The peer set — London City, Helsinki, Schiphol, Copenhagen, Barcelona El Prat — turns out to be a set of governance rules. London City removed final calls and paging in 2008;[^10] Helsinki followed in 2015. Schiphol tells deaf and blind passengers to notify their airline "at least 48 hours before departure,"[^10a] a pre-notification model that does not, on its face, satisfy 49 CFR Part 27. None of them publishes measured STI, LEQ, or intelligibility data. Their policies tell an airport what to write down. They do not tell it what to measure.

The measurements live in the fire code. NFPA 72 Chapter 24 sets the floor — STI ≥ 0.50 average per ADS, ≥ 0.45 at 90% of locations — and IEC 60268-16:2020 defines the STIPA protocol, run in the field with a talkbox and a calibrated meter.[^4] Virginia adopts NFPA 72 through its Uniform Statewide Building Code. But MWAA operates on federal land under a DOT lease, with its own Fire and Life Safety office administering compliance, so the applicable NFPA 72 edition and the AHJ arrangement are first-order verification items [VERIFY: AHJ arrangement and NFPA 72 edition on MWAA property].[^7a]

## Alarm fatigue: the mechanism transfers, the numbers do not

Hospitals learned this earlier and paid worse tuition. Boston Medical Center cut roughly a million monitor alarms a week to about 400,000. Johns Hopkins cut 63% of ICU alarms with one threshold change. The Joint Commission made alarm-fatigue reduction a National Patient Safety Goal because 86 to 99.5 percent of physiologic monitor alarms are false.[^14] A holdroom that pages constantly is running the alarm-fatigue experiment on its passengers, and the one message that finally matters arrives at ears that stopped listening two hours ago.

## Atlanta, December 17, 2017: the redundancy floor is federal

On a Sunday afternoon, Hartsfield-Jackson lost commercial power for roughly eleven hours. The FAA tower kept its power; the terminal did not. About 1,500 flights were grounded, and DOT later investigated some hundred tarmac delays.[^13a] A PA head-end, mass-notification server, and FIDS backbone sitting on the same commercial feed inherit that failure envelope. They need independent UPS and generator power, specified separately from the life-safety load, or the standard collapses on its first bad day.

The redundancy floor is federal. 49 CFR 27.71 requires federally assisted airports to display PA messages on video monitors.[^5] 14 CFR 382.53 puts the same obligation on the carriers, which pulls airline gate-podium and club audio into scope and disposes of any tenant carve-out. ADAAG 4.28.3 requires both audible and visual alarms, and for a physical reason: a visual signal can only be observed within the space it occupies.[^15] "Reduce PA, add screens" does not satisfy the floor. "Govern PA, harden every channel" does.

## The DCA overlay, honestly stated

On January 29, 2025, PSA/American 5342 and a U.S. Army UH-60L collided on final approach to DCA. Sixty-seven people died. The NTSB's final report, issued in February 2026, assigned probable cause to the FAA: a helicopter route placed too close to a runway approach. The FAA restricted helicopter operations within days, and that March it removed the offending segment of Route 4 from the charts.[^16]

Terminal-side communications were not implicated. Say that first. But at an airport where the accident's aftermath still shapes every conversation about safety messaging, "quiet by design" reads as an aesthetic decision. Frame the same standard as *intelligibility by design*, lead with the emergency-communication and accessibility tier, and its most publishable outcome becomes the ability to move a crowd with a voice when it matters — a frame that holds up on a stage that now includes the President and the Transportation Secretary.[^1]

## Concourse E: what the standard can and cannot reach

Concourse E opens in a few months: fourteen United gates, direct AeroTrain access, a 40,000-square-foot United Club, an $898M Tier 2 program carrying $235.8M in federal grants.[^17] A standard adopted in mid-2027 cannot govern its base build. It can govern the post-opening fit-outs, the instrumentation, and every solicitation not yet advertised. The urgency belongs to Concourse C/D and, downstream, to DCA Terminal 1.

Picture a winter IROPS evening. A ground stop hits, and the loudest instrument in the holdroom is not the MWAA head-end but a gate agent's handheld on speakerphone, layered over club audio, a TSA callout, and a bank of concession televisions. Two briefs in this run disagree on magnitude — the MWAA head-end carries either 30–40% or 60–70% of the audible surface [VERIFY: head-end share; no MWAA measurement exists] — and four agree on the point that matters: most of the audio in the room is not on MWAA's PA.[^18]

The tenant-systems chapter therefore has to distinguish three enforcement surfaces. New fit-outs can be reached at tenant-improvement permit and TCO. Sitting tenants can be reached only at renewal or through negotiated amendment. Airline gate operations can be reached through Use-and-Lease flow-down [VERIFY: U&L flow-down language].[^19] The SFO mechanism transfers — an audio-matrix modification plus a centralized announcement desk — but the headline does not: cite the 47% reduction in paging events and the 60% reduction in announcement duration, not the self-reported 40% noise reduction.[^7] Dubai's ten-month, airline-by-airline negotiation, which capped announcements at two per flight with no reported effect on on-time performance, marks the ceiling of what governance alone can do.[^20]

## The airlines will read it as MII

The highest-consequence unknown is whether the 2025 Use-and-Lease Agreement treats this amendment as a Majority-in-Interest voting item [VERIFY: 2025 U&L MII language].[^19] On principle, an amendment under standards-maintenance authority is not an MII matter. In practice, MII turns on whether the change materially increases rate-recoverable cost — and a standard requiring STIPA commissioning, head-end reprogramming, acoustic consultants on renovated projects, and re-verification after fit-outs is a rate-base cost driver. Denver's reported quiet-zone initiative was blocked by its dominant carrier — a single-source account, but directionally credible.[^21] IAD's dominant carrier is United. DCA's is American, at roughly 53% of year-end-2024 traffic, in a slot-controlled airport where a missed departure is a lost slot.[^22]

So write the consultation plan into the recommendation itself: an information item to the Airline Airport Affairs Committee, escalated to consultative on the diligence read. And draft the IROPS carve-out into the standard rather than around it: invoked by written declaration of the Airport Duty Manager during an active event, effective for the event's duration, reported monthly to the AAAC. Silence in the standard is the thing carriers will litigate.

## Timing, arithmetic, and the funding stack

The instrument is the Design Manual — seven volumes, 2020 edition, updated annually — and the target is the 2027 edition, authorized inside the current officer term.[^3] Timing is conditional on the C/D procurement status, which is not public [VERIFY: C/D procurement status]. Pre-advertisement, the standard enters as scope insertion. Mid-selection, as an RFP addendum. Under contract, as a change order with reported cost impact — at which point the urgency re-anchors to later C/D packages and DCA T1.

Acoustic ceilings run $5–$15 per square foot in new construction and $18–$45 in retrofit; on Concourse E's roughly 435,000 square feet at a 50% scope, avoided rework is $0.65M–$8.7M, or $1.3M–$17.4M full-area.[^8] A priced MWAA-wide stack — acoustic ceilings in mandatory zones, FIDS redundancy across roughly 200 gates, a mobile-notification platform — comes to $6M–$24M one-time, or about $40M once unpriced head-end reprogramming and commissioning are added. Annualized against IAD's 2025 enplanements, the priced range is $0.02–$0.09 per enplanement: 0.10 to 0.43 percent of projected 2030 signatory CPE.[^8]

The funding stack is not one bucket. Acoustic-ceiling and FIDS capital is GARB-financed and rate-recoverable through signatory CPE; the mobile-notification platform is PFC-eligible; commissioning is a project soft cost. Recurring O&M — STIPA re-verification, tenant-fit-out review, sound monitoring — runs $250K–$1.5M a year in the Engineering budget, plus 0.5–1.0 FTE if workload demands, and grant-funded scopes follow FAA grant-assurance notification. At the priced range, the cost sits inside the 2025A coverage margin; at the $40M envelope, Finance should confirm covenant impact before adoption. And the late-specification premium in commercial construction is 2× to 4× — a benchmarked figure, not the 1:10:100 heuristic.

## The Contrarian objection, answered

The strongest case against writing anything new goes like this: NFPA 72, ADA/ACAA, ACRP 175, and a commissioning specification already require all of it; the Design Manual may already carry acoustic language; the Board has not asked; and standards ossify while passenger technology matures.

Each objection is true in its own sentence. None is true across the seam. NFPA 72 governs the fire-alarm envelope, not the routine paging matrix or tenant audio. ACRP 175 recommends thresholds but binds no scope. The ADA sets a redundancy floor but no zoning grid. A commissioning spec tests one project. What the standard adds is integration and enforcement across the seam. As for Board demand: the July 2026 announcement is the Board's question — what the Authority will procure with $20 billion — and this is an answer. A Design Manual amendment on an annual cycle is reversible. A bond covenant is not.

## The decision: tiers, stops, and a 90-day plan

The Board should authorize the SVP for Engineering (President/CEO as principal) to develop the standard as a Design Manual amendment: an information item to the Business Administration Committee, then a Board resolution, under existing standards-maintenance authority, with no new appropriation. The first 90 days are already legible. Catalogue the 2020 Manual's acoustic and PA language in Volumes 1, 4, and 5 (Days 1–14). Engage an on-call acoustic consultant under an existing IDIQ (Days 15–45). Convene a working group across Engineering, Airline Affairs, Public Safety, Operations and Emergency Management, and IT (Days 45–75). Deliver the outline, cost estimate, and airline-consultation plan at Day 90.[^12]

The standard itself is three tiers in one instrument, each with an owner. **Mandatory at both airports**, owned by Fire and Life Safety: the NFPA 72 / ACRP 175 intelligibility floor, STIPA-verified occupied at TCO and again after envelope-penetrating fit-outs; redundant channels — audible, visual/digital, staffed — for every safety-critical, boarding-critical, and IROPS message; independent generator and UPS power for the ECS head-end and PA backbone; and the federal accessibility floor (49 CFR 27.71, 14 CFR 382.53, ADAAG 4.28.3) written in as design criteria.[^13] **Recommended, context permitting**, owned by Operations with Emergency Management concurring: zoned PA with pre-authorized override; tenant audio governance reaching gate podiums and concessions; hearing loops at gates and counters, on the Port Authority of NY & NJ precedent;[^23] sensory rooms at each hub — Pittsburgh, Atlanta, and Indianapolis have them, and IAD is behind;[^24] and centralized non-routine announcements on the SFO model. **Context-dependent**, owned by Engineering: DCA gets a higher operational announcement floor, because slot loss is unrecoverable and post-January-29 safety-messaging load is elevated; IAD gets a higher accessibility and language-support floor, because 10.53 million of its 29.01 million 2025 passengers were international.[^25] Variances are adjudicated by the SVP for Engineering, with Public Safety concurring wherever safety messaging is implicated, and publish quarterly to the AAAC.

Two stop conditions travel with the authorization. If the diligence read finds NFPA-72-equivalent binding language already in the 2020 Manual, staff returns with a re-scoped enforcement-and-tenant-reach amendment. If either dominant carrier formally objects under U&L consultation, flow-down pauses until adjudicated at the AAAC or the Board. Beyond Day 90: Concourse E gets STIPA baselines within 12 months of opening, and its tenant fit-outs are reviewed against the criteria in year one. MWAA should also publish its baseline and post-implementation STIPA and paging-density data — closing the measurement gap the peer canon leaves open.

If the standard is not in the C/D scope at the next unadvertised solicitation, it will be argued back in later as a change order, at a cost multiple this article does not need to repeat. That is a decision, not a study. The Board's window is now.

